# Exam track — Theme: Describing a Picture — Sub-lesson 3: Weather Description
#
# The WÉINI task for the Christmas-market photo of sub-lessons 1–2: the time of
# day, and a hedged guess at what kind of day this is. The examiner asks it on
# almost every picture, and it is the one place in this theme where the candidate
# is allowed to INFER rather than report — so the point of this file is the
# hedging vocabulary, not the weather words.
#
# This photo's inference angle is new relative to the other three: it does not
# ask season (the decorations already answer that) or weather (the sky gives
# little to read — grey and overcast, no rain, no strong light or dark). What it
# DOES give the candidate to reason with is: (1) festive lights and garlands are
# lit despite a bright, un-dark sky — so is this evening or daytime?, and (2) a
# crowd of people and children gathered at a food stall — is that an ordinary day
# or a special one?
#
# Sub-lesson (2) is the direct mirror of groussgaass/03's negative-evidence frame
# (`Et ass warscheinlech net Chrëschtdag, well et keng Luuchten … ginn.`): there
# the ABSENCE of lights argued against Christmas; here the PRESENCE of lights and
# garlands argues for it. A learner who has played both themes must read the
# photo's actual evidence rather than recall a fixed answer — the same payoff the
# groussgaass/Schueberfouer summer/winter contrast was built for.
#
# Sub-lesson (1) inverts groussgaass's OTHER hedge frame: there, "a lot of people
# on the way" was evidence for an ORDINARY weekday; here the same evidence (a
# crowd, but gathered at a market stall rather than walking through) argues for a
# SPECIAL day. Same observable fact, opposite conclusion depending on what the
# people are doing — a genuinely new angle, not a rename of the groussgaass frame.
#
# The photo detail this describes: a pale grey, overcast daytime sky (no sun, no
# darkness), the stall's string lights and garlands lit regardless, no rain, dry
# ground, everyone in winter coats — evidence for cold rather than for a
# particular time of day.
#
# PURE DESCRIPTION, same as 01 and 02 — no @question prompts, no first-person
# opinion. A hedged inference *about the photo* ("I think it is Christmas Day,
# because the lights are on") is not an opinion: it is an observation with a
# confidence marker, and a stranger can still check it against the picture.
# Nothing about the learner's own feelings about Christmas or markets belongs
# here, and nothing of that shape was authored.
#
# Same A1–A2 constraint as the rest of the theme: present tense, simple main
# clauses or short two-clause sentences, no attributive adjectives on masculine
# or neuter nouns.
#
# Words sub-lessons 1–2 already teach (d'Bild, d'Leit, d'Luuchten, gesinn,
# de Chrëschtmaart, d'Stad, …) are deliberately NOT repeated — the pass-gate
# makes them prerequisites and stat keys are shared across the app.
#
# Ten @fill blocks — this theme's highest-value frames, because a hedge is a
# pattern that transfers to every picture and every topic. All six hedges the
# samples use appear across this theme's three files; here `Ech mengen, …` and
# `Et ass warscheinlech …` are reused (the two hedges Schueberfouer already
# owns) on the NEW positive Christmas-evidence claim rather than a season guess,
# `Et gesäit no … aus`, `Ech sinn net sécher, mee villäicht` and `Et kéint … sinn`
# carry the day-type and near-holiday inferences, and `Ech weess net, ob …`
# drills the uncertain-`ob` pattern on a checkable visual fact. See the skill's
# references/content-contract.md for the mechanized bounds.

@lesson P3.03 "Chrëschtmaart: Weather Description"

# Same photo as 01, declared again: @image is per-file, and the learner must see
# the photo during THIS sub-lesson too. The alt text differs on purpose — it
# describes the detail this file drills.
@image "/assets/exam/picture/chreschtmaart/img/chreschtmaart.webp"
@image-alt "The same Christmas-market photo, seen for the time of day: a pale grey overcast daytime sky, the stall's string lights and fir garlands lit despite the daylight, dry ground, no rain, everyone in winter coats."

# --- Weather and sky ---
@word d'Wieder = the weather
@word den Himmel = the sky
@word d'Sonn = the sun
@word d'Wollek = the cloud
@word d'Wolleken = the clouds
@word gro = grey
@word bedeckt = overcast
@word reenen = to rain
@word schneien = to snow

# --- Time and season words ---
@word den Dag = the day
@word den Owend = the evening
@word d'Woch = the week
@word de Chrëschtmount = December
@word d'Chrëschtdeeg = the Christmas holidays
@word de Chrëschtdag = Christmas Day
@word blénken = to twinkle, to sparkle
@word no = near, close

# --- Hedging a guess ---
@word mengen = to think
@word warscheinlech = probably
@word villäicht = maybe
@word sécher = sure
@word well = because
@word wëssen = to know
@word kafen = to buy

# --- Seasons (contrast for the hedges) ---
@word de Wanter = the winter
@word de Summer = the summer
@word d'Fréijoer = the spring
@word den Hierscht = the autumn

@sentence
@lu Den Himmel ass gro a bedeckt.
@en The sky is grey and overcast.
@distractor-lu blo
@distractor-lu Sonn
@distractor-en blue
@distractor-en sun

@sentence
@lu D'Wieder ass kal, mee et reent net.
@en The weather is cold, but it is not raining.
@distractor-lu waarm
@distractor-lu Wollek
@distractor-en warm
@distractor-en cloud

@sentence
@lu D'Luuchten um Stand blénken.
@en The lights at the stall are sparkling.
@distractor-lu schéngen
@distractor-lu Beem
@distractor-en shine
@distractor-en trees

@sentence
@lu Ech mengen, et ass den Dag, net den Owend.
@en I think it is daytime, not evening.
@distractor-lu nuets
@distractor-lu Wieder
@distractor-en at night
@distractor-en weather

@sentence
@lu De Chrëschtmaart ass am Chrëschtmount.
@en The Christmas market is in December.
@distractor-lu Summer
@distractor-lu Stad
@distractor-en summer
@distractor-en city

@sentence
@lu Et ass warscheinlech kal um Chrëschtmaart.
@en It is probably cold at the Christmas market.
@distractor-lu waarm
@distractor-lu Wieder
@distractor-en warm
@distractor-en weather

# --- Reusable frames (@fill) ---

# The positive mirror of groussgaass's negative-evidence frame: there the
# ABSENCE of lights argued against Christmas, here their PRESENCE argues for it.
# Two blanks in different clauses (main clause noun, then the well-clause noun),
# so they cannot swap (R6). The determiner `d'` stays fixed before the second
# blank, ruling out a masculine tile there on grammar alone (R4); `well` keeps
# the verb `sinn` at the end, an order the learner reads rather than assembles.
@fill
@lu Ech mengen, et ass [Chrëschtdag], well d'[Luuchten] un sinn.
@en I think it is [Christmas Day], because the [lights] are on.
@distractor-lu Summer
@distractor-lu Fréijoer
@distractor-lu Wieder
@distractor-en summer
@distractor-en spring
@distractor-en weather

# The new "many people" inversion: the same observable fact groussgaass used as
# evidence for an ORDINARY day here argues for a SPECIAL one, because the people
# are gathered at a market stall rather than walking through. `vill` stays fixed
# and forces a plural, so a singular tile fails on agreement as well as on sense.
@fill
@lu Et ass warscheinlech net en normalen Dag, well vill [Leit] um Maart sinn.
@en It is probably not a normal day, because a lot of [people] are at the market.
@distractor-lu Wolleken
@distractor-lu Beem
@distractor-en clouds
@distractor-en trees

# The split `gesäit … aus` hedge, contrasted with a `mee`-clause — the frame
# teaches that a scene can look one way and the checkable weather fact can still
# differ. The comma before the blank both marks the clause boundary and exempts
# it from the Eifeler Regel.
@fill
@lu Et gesäit no engem kalen Chrëschtmaart aus, mee d'Wieder ass [gro].
@en It looks like a cold Christmas market, but the weather is [grey].
@distractor-lu sonneg
@distractor-lu waarm
@distractor-en sunny
@distractor-en warm

# The `no sinn` proximity frame — "the holidays are near" is the shape of
# reasoning that transfers to any pre-holiday photo. One blank, and the fixed
# noun before it ends in a non-`-n` consonant, so no comma is needed for R5.
# Distractors are adjectives that cannot coherently describe a date drawing
# closer, so they fail on sense rather than on grammar (R3).
@fill
@lu Vill Leit sinn um Chrëschtmaart, well d'Chrëschtdeeg [no] sinn.
@en A lot of people are at the Christmas market, because the Christmas holidays are [near].
@distractor-lu midd
@distractor-lu glécklech
@distractor-en tired
@distractor-en happy

# Hedge: `Ech sinn net sécher, mee villäicht` — the strongest disclaimer in the
# set, used here for a guess the photo only weakly supports (the sky gives
# little to read). The comma both marks the clause boundary and exempts the
# blank from the Eifeler Regel. Season nouns are the blank; both distractors are
# seasons the coats and lit decorations contradict.
@fill
@lu Ech sinn net sécher, mee villäicht ass et [Wanter].
@en I am not sure, but maybe it is [winter].
@distractor-lu Summer
@distractor-lu Fréijoer
@distractor-en summer
@distractor-en spring

# Hedge: `Et kéint … sinn`, the subjunctive hedge, with the verb at the end —
# exactly the order a learner should read rather than assemble. Two blanks in
# DIFFERENT CLAUSES (the second inside the `well`-clause), so they cannot swap
# (R6). `der` stays fixed before the first blank, admitting only a feminine
# tile — masculine `Chrëschtmount` is out on grammar alone (R4).
@fill
@lu Et kéint eng normal [Woch] sinn, well d'Leit [Iessen] kafen.
@en It could be a normal [week], because the people are buying [food].
@distractor-lu Chrëschtmount
@distractor-lu Wieder
@distractor-lu Beem
@distractor-en December
@distractor-en weather
@distractor-en trees

# The uncertain-`ob` pattern, drilled here on a different verb than groussgaass's
# rain guess: whether it is going to snow is exactly the kind of thing a
# stranger can verify against a grey winter sky. `ob` sends the verb to the end,
# an order the learner reads rather than assembles; the comma exempts the blank
# right after it from the Eifeler Regel.
@fill
@lu Ech weess net, ob et [schneit].
@en I do not know whether it is [snowing].
@distractor-lu schéngt
@distractor-lu blénkt
@distractor-en shining
@distractor-en sparkling

# The two-clause weather report from Schueberfouer, reused with this photo's
# actual sky. Two blanks in different clauses and different word classes
# (adjective, then noun), so they cannot swap.
@fill
@lu D'Wieder ass [gro], an ech gesinn keng [Sonn].
@en The weather is [grey], and I see no [sun].
@distractor-lu sonneg
@distractor-lu Wollek
@distractor-en sunny
@distractor-en cloud

# The `Ech mengen, …` opener reused on the theme's OTHER new inference — time of
# day rather than season, since the lit decorations make that the harder of the
# two questions this photo raises. Comma exempts the blank from the Eifeler
# Regel; both distractors are times of day the daylight sky contradicts.
@fill
@lu Ech mengen, et ass [Dag].
@en I think it is [daytime].
@distractor-lu Owend
@distractor-lu Nuecht
@distractor-en evening
@distractor-en night
